    "title": "April full pink moon to rise in Texas skies Wednesday evening",
    "text": "Despite its name, Wednesday evening's full moon won't actually appear pink in the night sky.Sara Diggins/American-StatesmanApril is bringing a little extra glow to the Texas night sky. That's right — sky watchers in the Lone Star State and beyond are in for treat this evening, as this month's full \"pink\" moon is set to peak at approximately 9:11 p.m., according to Time and Date. Article continues below this adGet Digital Access and Stay Informed With Trusted Local News.Get Digital Access and Stay Informed With Trusted Local News.ONLY 25¢Will the full moon actually appear pink? Despite its name, the full moon won't actually appear pink in the night sky.Instead, the celestial spectacle will glow in its typical silver hue. According to the Old Farmer's Almanac, April's full \"pink\" moon actually gets its name from a wildflower native to eastern North America that blooms in early spring, called \"phlox subulata\" — otherwise known as \"moss pink.\"This flower was often in full bloom by the time April rolled around, which is why this month's full moon is often referred to as the full \"pink\" moon.Article continues below this adWhen can Texans catch the best view?Express-News meteorologist Anthony Franze says while there may be some cloud cover, there are plenty of opportunities to peek at the full pink moon.Article continues below this ad\"Across South and Central Texas, partly cloudy skies are expected between 8 and 11 p.m. So while clouds will likely get in the way at times, you should still be able to catch some glimpses of the full pink moon,\" his report reads.\"After 11 p.m., skies are likely to become overcast across the region, likely obscuring the view of the moon.\"Article continues below this adSan Antonio Express News LogoStart your day with the San Antonio news you need to know.",
